2. Significant Accomplishments

2.1 Java Exploration

The Java language offers a number of significant potential advantages
for use in our context. Like Ada, it is much safer than C or C++
because of its built-in memory management and it's lack of pointers.
Also like Ada, it is a concurrent language, with a built-in thread
library. It has also proven portable and distributable, with
well-conceived and robust mechanisms for migrating code across
platforms. Finally, a huge investment is being made in industry in
software development environments, class libraries, and applications.
We can leverage this investment.

There are a number of key questions that need to be addressed. First,
since the language is conceived around interpreted byte code, will it
be capable of delivering the performance needed by signal processing
applications? We have made some preliminary measurements, comparing
interpreted Java code against compiled Java code using a just-in-time
Java compiler, and found about a factor of 30 improvement in speed. The
next step will be to compare against optimized C code. Of course, Java
compilers and interpreters will improve over time, as there is a huge
investment in them in industry. Moreover, aside from the memory
management, there is no reason that compiled Java should be any slower
than C or C++. The benefits of run-time memory management, however, are
considerable, so a reasonable performance penalty will be an acceptable
cost.

A second key question is whether the Java thread library provides an
adequate platform for constructing concurrent applications.  The basic
mechanism, wait and notify, is too low level for most applications
programming.  In particular, direct use of such low-level primitives
makes validation of concurrent programs very difficult.  For example,
there is no direct way of ensuring determinacy.

We have completed two pilot projects that show that higher-level
determinate mechanisms can be built on top of Java threads. In the
first of these, Dick Stevens, a visiting scholar from the Naval
Research Labs, together with two graduate students, Marlene Wan and
Peggy Laramie, have designed a set of classes that implement Kahn
process networks. KPNs have sequential processes that communicate via
unidirectional FIFO queues. Based on previous work at Berkeley and at
Lincoln Labs by Tom Parks, they were able to implement a simple
algorithm that ensures that the size of the FIFO queues will not grow
unbounded unless the program requires them to grow unbounded.

The second project, completed by Jim Young of the CAD group at Berkeley,
implements a barrier synchronization mechanism on top of Java threads.

These two pilot projects are very encouraging because the two concurrency
models are very different from one another, and yet both appear to be
efficiently implementable on top of Java threads.  The next step, of
course, is to quantify the efficiency.

If this initial exploration continues encouraging, our plan is to model
a Java class library after key elements of the Ptolemy kernel. This
will enable construction of lightweight, distributable applications
that use the style of Ptolemy, and will enable leveraging the Ptolemy
infrastructure (schedulers, user interfaces, etc).


2.2 An Object-Oriented and Scripted Interface to Ptolemy

We are designing a class hierarchy in Itcl (an object-oriented
extension of the Tcl scripting language) that we expect will eventually
replace ptcl as the main interface to the Ptolemy kernel. Its key
features are that it will provide essentially full access to kernel
functionality from Tcl (unlike ptcl's rather more limited access) and
that it will be object-oriented.  By building this scripted layer of
abstraction, we expect to be able to perform much more sophisticated
experiments with dynamic reconfiguration of Ptolemy applications.


2.3 Process-Level Type System

Rajagopal Nagarajan, from Imperial College in England, visited our
group for one week of intensive collaboration on formalisms that might
be applicable to our notion of process-level type systems.  He
introduced us to Interaction Categories, an approach to classifying
process types in terms of histories of events.  We have compared the
IC formalism against our own tagged signal model, and have written a
paper to be submitted to the IEEE Transactions on CAD.  We are working
on arranging for Raja to join our group as a postdoc.


2.4 Infrastructure Improvements

Christopher Hylands built a simple syntax-sensitive Java editor in Tycho
and an interface between Tycho and a Java applet viewer.  Tycho is an
extensible user-interface toolkit designed as part of the Ptolemy project.


2.5 Multidimensional Signal Processing (contract Option, Questech, Inc)

We are completing negotiations with Thomson CSF and Questech to get
