Task Description

Formal models, design methodology, and a suite of software tools are
being developed to support the design of distributed adaptive signal
processing systems. Such systems in defense applications will be
primarily reactive. These systems react continuously to their
environment at the speed of the environment. They have hard real-time
constraints, and are frequently safety-critical, to the point that
failures could result in loss of human life. Reactive systems may need
to be distributed for several reasons. First, such systems usually
include sensors and actuators as an integral part, and these sensors
and actuators cannot be arbitrarily co-located. Second, systems can be
made more survivable and robust if they are distributed. Third, such
systems are often used in command, control, and communication, where
the people involved are geographically dispersed. Fourth, meeting the
real-time constraints may require parallel processing. Finally, power
and size constraints limit the processing on mobile and remote-sensing
platforms.

Robust distributed reactive systems must be capable of adapting to
changing conditions. Computing resources and sensors may appear and
disappear from the network. Quality of service demands may change as
conditions change. Even the list of required services may change over
time. The system is therefore continuously being redesigned while it
operates, and all the while it must not fail.

This project aims to adapt systematic and formal approaches that have
proven effective for small-scale systems to large-scale systems. This
will be accomplished by using hierarchy and information hiding to make
the large scale systems appear to be small at any given level of the
hierarchy. Standard languages such as C, C++, and Java will be used at
the detail level. The software tools will be built in a modular way so
that critical components (such as scheduling and verification tools
necessary to support safety-critical design) can accompany the deployed
system into the field. This will enable adaptation and robustness.

The specific objectives are:

 - Software architecture for modular deployable design tools.
 - Domain-specific tools for distributed adaptive signal processing.
 - Models of computation for dynamically configured systems.
 - Theory and implementation of a process-level type system.
 - Methodologies for formal analysis and testing of dynamic systems.
 - System-level visualization.
 - Array-based design of multidimensional signal processing.

